  "summary": "The health-tech sector is seeing significant capital activity and regulatory shifts today. Forus, a digital health platform, secured $150 million in Series C funding at a $3 billion valuation from a consortium led by Bain Capital Ventures, while GenHealth.ai raised $16.5 million to expand its healthcare AI agents, reflecting continued investor confidence in AI-driven healthcare solutions. On the regulatory front, the FDA appointed permanent heads to its drug and vaccine centers to bring stability after recent leadership departures, though the agency also issued a warning to an active pharmaceutical ingredients supplier citing facility maintenance issues. The biotech pipeline shows mixed results, with AstraZeneca posting strong late-stage COPD trial data and Roivant hitting mid-stage lung disease study goals, but Novartis faced its second major trial failure in days on a neuromuscular drug, adding pressure to the company. Healthcare providers and tech companies are increasingly grappling with AI integration challenges, as coverage expands on how to measure AI's value for clinical staff beyond administrative tasks like charting, while also establishing proper safeguards for agentic AI systems in patient care settings. Finally, access challenges persist for newly approved treatments—a new pancreatic cancer drug faced administrative delays despite FDA approval—and tobacco regulation issues emerged as Reynolds began selling unauthorized fruity vape products, potentially encouraging other manufacturers to test FDA boundaries.",
